    "articles": [
    {
      "id": "created-as-his-workmanship",
      "slug": "created-as-his-workmanship",
      "status": "draft",
        "data": {
          "title": "Created as His Workmanship: The Meaning of Poiema",
          "author": "Pastor Laurence Windham",
          "published_at": "2026-08-15T12:00:00.000Z",
          "excerpt": "Why Paul chose the word 'poiema' in Ephesians 2:10, and what it means for our redemption and daily callings as God's poetic masterpieces.",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "a1",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "as1",
                  "text": "In Ephesians 2:10, the apostle Paul writes: 'For we are His workmanship (ποίημα, poiema), created in Christ Jesus for good works, which God prepared beforehand that we should walk in them.' We are not haphazard products of fate, nor are we mere cog-like functionaries. The Greek word poiema signifies a crafted work of art, a poem, an intentional masterpiece."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "a2",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "as2",
                  "text": "When we gather at Poiema Retreats, our longing is to rediscover what it means to be living expressions of God’s grace in our generation. When believers step away from the relentless churn of cultural performance and rest together in Christ, the beauty of that divine workmanship becomes unmistakably clear."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      },
    {
      "id": "living-together-in-an-age-of-loneliness",
      "slug": "living-together-in-an-age-of-loneliness",
      "status": "draft",
        "data": {
          "title": "Living Together in an Age of Loneliness",
          "author": "Elder David Cooper",
          "published_at": "2026-07-20T12:00:00.000Z",
          "excerpt": "Christian friendship is not an optional hobby. Biblical communion requires intentional time, shared tables, and deep mutual affection.",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "c1",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "cs1",
                  "text": "Modern life connects us to thousands through glowing screens, yet leaves us uniquely starved for real communion. Dietrich Bonhoeffer reminded us that Christian brotherhood is not an ideal which we must realize, but a reality created by God in Christ in which we may participate."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "c2",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "cs2",
                  "text": "This is why the retreat format matters so much. A weekend around a common table, breaking bread and singing psalms without urgency, reminds our souls of what we were built for."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ],
    "pages": [
      {
        "id": "home",
        "slug": "home",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "ποίημα — Poiema Retreats",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "h1",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "hs1",
                  "text": "The purpose of our retreats is to inform, encourage, and inspire Christian young adults regarding who they are in Christ. We see this as a life-awareness that is essential for everyone’s spiritual walk as well as the wellspring of joy and confidence. After all, we are God’s workmanship (poiema) and we want that to set in, be real to us, and when it does: worldview, apologetics, theology, vocation, and, well…all of life is set and in perspective."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
